1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,167
Kia ora, students.

2
00:00:01,167 --> 00:00:02,625
This video is by Akshit Verma,

3
00:00:02,625 --> 00:00:04,792
for INFO 7375,

4
00:00:04,792 --> 00:00:06,292
week one, and the voice you are

5
00:00:06,292 --> 00:00:07,658
hearing is synthetic.

6
00:00:07,833 --> 00:00:09,542
One concept from chapter one:

7
00:00:09,542 --> 00:00:11,333
what pretraining actually targets.

8
00:00:11,333 --> 00:00:12,917
Not a summary of the chapter.

9
00:00:12,917 --> 00:00:15,033
One idea, shown end to end.

10
00:00:15,208 --> 00:00:16,792
Here is the claim in one breath.

11
00:00:16,792 --> 00:00:18,333
Training does not teach a model what

12
00:00:18,333 --> 00:00:20,742
is true. It teaches it what followed.

13
00:00:20,833 --> 00:00:22,000
I am going to show you that

14
00:00:22,000 --> 00:00:23,867
happening, with numbers you can rerun.

15
00:00:24,583 --> 00:00:26,083
The chapter states the loop in four

16
00:00:26,083 --> 00:00:28,000
steps. Take a passage of text.

17
00:00:28,000 --> 00:00:29,208
Hide the next token.

18
00:00:29,208 --> 00:00:31,250
Ask the model for its distribution.

19
00:00:31,250 --> 00:00:32,958
Compare that distribution with the token

20
00:00:32,958 --> 00:00:34,417
that actually followed.

21
00:00:34,417 --> 00:00:36,250
Then nudge every parameter so the token

22
00:00:36,250 --> 00:00:38,575
that followed gets a little more probability.

23
00:00:38,625 --> 00:00:39,958
Nothing in that loop is told what

24
00:00:39,958 --> 00:00:40,825
is true.

25
00:00:41,083 --> 00:00:42,208
So I built a corpus where the

26
00:00:42,208 --> 00:00:43,783
frequent answer is false.

27
00:00:43,833 --> 00:00:45,583
This is constructed — I invented it,

28
00:00:45,583 --> 00:00:46,458
it is not web text,

29
00:00:46,458 --> 00:00:48,117
and it says so on screen.

30
00:00:48,250 --> 00:00:50,500
One context: the capital of Australia is.

31
00:00:50,500 --> 00:00:52,375
Sydney follows seven times.

32
00:00:52,375 --> 00:00:53,583
Canberra, the true answer,

33
00:00:53,583 --> 00:00:56,033
follows three. Melbourne never follows.

34
00:00:56,458 --> 00:00:58,333
Training starts with the weights at zero,

35
00:00:58,333 --> 00:01:00,333
so every token is equally likely.

36
00:01:00,333 --> 00:01:01,958
Point three three three each.

37
00:01:01,958 --> 00:01:03,583
The loss is one point zero nine

38
00:01:03,583 --> 00:01:05,292
eight six — that is the natural log

39
00:01:05,292 --> 00:01:07,542
of three, exactly what uniform over three

40
00:01:07,542 --> 00:01:09,950
options costs. Now run the loop.

41
00:01:10,167 --> 00:01:11,083
Watch the bars.

42
00:01:11,083 --> 00:01:12,958
Step one, Sydney is already ahead.

43
00:01:12,958 --> 00:01:14,500
Step five, point five six.

44
00:01:14,500 --> 00:01:16,417
Step twenty, point six seven.

45
00:01:16,417 --> 00:01:18,250
By step six hundred it settles:

46
00:01:18,250 --> 00:01:19,750
Sydney point six nine nine,

47
00:01:19,750 --> 00:01:21,250
Canberra point two nine nine,

48
00:01:21,250 --> 00:01:23,167
Melbourne point zero zero two.

49
00:01:23,167 --> 00:01:24,625
The loss fell the whole way.

50
00:01:24,625 --> 00:01:26,117
Nothing checked a fact.

51
00:01:26,333 --> 00:01:27,750
Here is the whole concept in two

52
00:01:27,750 --> 00:01:30,375
numbers. Distance from the corpus frequencies:

53
00:01:30,375 --> 00:01:32,500
zero point zero zero two two.

54
00:01:32,500 --> 00:01:33,708
Distance from the truth:

55
00:01:33,708 --> 00:01:35,833
zero point seven zero one one.

56
00:01:35,833 --> 00:01:37,667
The objective walked the model to within

57
00:01:37,667 --> 00:01:39,042
two thousandths of the corpus,

58
00:01:39,042 --> 00:01:40,833
and left it seven tenths away from

59
00:01:40,833 --> 00:01:42,000
the right answer.

60
00:01:42,000 --> 00:01:43,408
Argmax is Sydney.

61
00:01:43,708 --> 00:01:45,458
One more thing the numbers show.

62
00:01:45,458 --> 00:01:47,333
The loss stops at zero point six

63
00:01:47,333 --> 00:01:48,125
one three one.

64
00:01:48,125 --> 00:01:49,083
It cannot reach zero,

65
00:01:49,083 --> 00:01:51,083
because the corpus contradicts itself about

66
00:01:51,083 --> 00:01:53,458
this context. The floor is the corpus's

67
00:01:53,458 --> 00:01:56,208
own entropy, zero point six one zero

68
00:01:56,208 --> 00:01:57,667
nine. A perfect fit to a wrong

69
00:01:57,667 --> 00:01:59,825
corpus is still a perfect fit.

70
00:02:00,083 --> 00:02:01,500
You might think the later training stage

71
00:02:01,500 --> 00:02:03,708
fixes this. The chapter is blunt:

72
00:02:03,708 --> 00:02:05,708
neither stage receives an answer key.

73
00:02:05,708 --> 00:02:07,833
Preference tuning targets what raters approved

74
00:02:07,833 --> 00:02:10,083
of. Approval and correctness overlap,

75
00:02:10,083 --> 00:02:11,792
but where they diverge the signal follows

76
00:02:11,792 --> 00:02:13,542
approval. A confident,

77
00:02:13,542 --> 00:02:15,917
well organized, wrong answer is exactly what

78
00:02:15,917 --> 00:02:16,992
gets preferred.

79
00:02:17,333 --> 00:02:19,292
Here is what this does not establish.

80
00:02:19,292 --> 00:02:21,783
My corpus is three tokens and invented.

81
00:02:21,833 --> 00:02:23,042
This does not show that a real

82
00:02:23,042 --> 00:02:25,708
model's probabilities equal web frequencies —

83
00:02:25,708 --> 00:02:27,250
real models generalize,

84
00:02:27,250 --> 00:02:28,833
and the chapter says nobody can read

85
00:02:28,833 --> 00:02:30,867
off why a given prediction was made.

86
00:02:31,042 --> 00:02:32,583
It also does not show models are

87
00:02:32,583 --> 00:02:34,792
usually wrong. Only that truth was never

88
00:02:34,792 --> 00:02:35,783
the target.

89
00:02:36,000 --> 00:02:37,333
Your turn. The script is in the

90
00:02:37,333 --> 00:02:39,417
folder. Change Sydney's count from seven

91
00:02:39,417 --> 00:02:41,075
to two and rerun it.

92
00:02:41,083 --> 00:02:43,417
Watch argmax flip to Canberra — not because

93
00:02:43,417 --> 00:02:44,750
the model learned a fact,

94
00:02:44,750 --> 00:02:46,533
but because you edited the corpus.

95
00:02:47,500 --> 00:02:48,750
What pretraining actually targets:

96
00:02:48,750 --> 00:02:50,083
the token that followed,

97
00:02:50,083 --> 00:02:51,000
not the truth.

98
00:02:51,000 --> 00:02:53,292
This video was created by Akshit Verma

99
00:02:53,292 --> 00:02:56,042
for INFO 7375,

100
00:02:56,042 --> 00:02:58,408
Prompt Engineering for Generative AI.

